@NumbuTruth The office is so great cause it means accountability. Like I said the only people crying about RTO are the ones who have been pushing their work off on others or just not doing it. When everyone is in the office it becomes clear who is actually working and getting their job done.

Because I basically had to do mandatory 100 h weeks no overtime remote trying to get through everyone’s crap without any respect from anyone. Even when it wasn’t my job and I’d see photos of the other remote workers on the beach or chilling while calling me lazy when I asked for help. It was infuriating. I’d gladly take 80 h because that means I’d get more than 4 h sleep. 

I think you are proving yourself to be one of the under performers by how you can’t read though.

@NoECRAMthat is absolutely not true and - I’m shocked how people so sure in something they don’t quite understand but continue to spread their misunderstanding 

Taking the house owning analogy, offering stock during IPO is nothing like renting, and more of “offering to buy a closet room while keep rights to own the rest of the house and how it’s handled”. You still get to buy and own the closet room - that is what stock is. 

Insta liquidation event had a meaning of “cashing out”: before IPO, previous investors only own the house (rooms, furniture, utensils, etc) and no cash - but maybe they dream of spending all they have on girls and cars? If house is not sold on open market all they have is a house, during IPO they can offer part of the house to be sold to get that sweet cash (I.e. liquidate -> exchange ownership for money) . If no exchange rules existed - a LOT of investors would just sell the whole thing - and not care how the maintenance will be handled later, although they have the most experience and knowledge how to maintain it.
